%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: reflective teaching
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: What is the primary goal of reflective teaching?
    Options:
    - To prove one's teaching methods are correct
    - To improve student learning and professional practice
    - To complete administrative paperwork
    - To compare oneself favorably with colleagues
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a key characteristic of a reflective
      teacher?
    Options:
    - Strict adherence to a prescribed curriculum
    - Reliance on intuition without analysis
    - Critical examination of one's own beliefs and practices
    - Avoidance of new teaching methods
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The "What? So What? Now What?" model is a framework for:'
    Options:
    - Lesson planning
    - Student assessment
    - Classroom management
    - Structured reflection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'Maintaining a reflective journal primarily serves to:'
    Options:
    - Fulfill a course requirement
    - Provide a record for school inspection
    - Document experiences and facilitate self-dialogue
    - Share all personal thoughts with the principal
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'In the context of reflective teaching, ''critical incident analysis''
      refers to:'
    Options:
    - Analyzing a student's failure
    - Reflecting on a significant classroom event to derive meaning
    - Criticizing a colleague's teaching method
    - Investigating a disciplinary issue
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which strategy involves teachers collaboratively planning, observing,
      and refining a single lesson?
    Options:
    - Peer coaching
    - Lesson study
    - Micro-teaching
    - Workshop
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: A teacher analyzing video recordings of their own teaching is using
      which strategy for reflection?
    Options:
    - Action research
    - Journaling
    - Direct observation and analysis
    - Portfolio development
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The role of a mentor in promoting reflective teaching is best
      described as a:'
    Options:
    - Evaluator
    - Facilitator who asks probing questions
    - Source of all correct answers
    - Disciplinarian
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following questions is MOST likely to promote reflective
      thinking?
    Options:
    - '"Did you cover the syllabus today?"'
    - '"Will you use the same activity tomorrow?"'
    - '"How many students passed the test?"'
    - '"Why do you think your explanation of that concept was effective for some
      students but not others?"'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'Reflective teaching is best understood as a:'
    Options:
    - Linear process with a clear end point
    - Cyclical and ongoing process
    - One-time activity at the end of a term
    - Theoretical concept with no practical application
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: If a teacher uses student feedback to redesign a unit plan, they
      are engaging in which phase of the reflective cycle?
    Options:
    - Description
    - Analysis
    - Action Planning
    - Evaluation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A school allocates specific time every week for teachers to discuss
      student work and teaching challenges. This initiative is promoting:'
    Options:
    - Administrative compliance
    - Collaborative reflection
    - Independent planning
    - Parent-teacher communication
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is the LEAST effective way to promote a
      reflective culture in a school?
    Options:
    - Encouraging risk-taking and viewing mistakes as learning opportunities
    - Prioritizing high-stakes test scores as the only measure of success
    - Implementing structured peer observation programs
    - Providing training on reflective models and frameworks
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher educator asks student-teachers to write about a time
      a lesson did not go as planned and what they learned from it. This is an example
      of using:'
    Options:
    - A standardized test
    - A reflective portfolio
    - Critical incident analysis
    - Summative assessment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The main purpose of a teaching portfolio in reflective practice
      is to:'
    Options:
    - Showcase only successful lessons and achievements
    - Provide a comprehensive and curated record of professional growth over time
    - Serve as a public relations tool for the school
    - Replace all other forms of assessment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'According to Dewey, reflection is triggered by:'
    Options:
    - A state of certainty
    - Receiving positive feedback
    - Following instructions precisely
    - A state of confusion or doubt
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which strategy involves a teacher systematically investigating
      a problem in their own classroom?
    Options:
    - Peer observation
    - Action research
    - Lesson study
    - Journaling
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: A reflective teacher, after a lesson, considers how the cultural
      backgrounds of students might have influenced their engagement. This demonstrates
      reflection at which level?
    Options:
    - Technical (focus on skills)
    - Practical (focus on assumptions and student meaning)
    - Critical (focus on ethical and social context)
    - Superficial (focus on surface events)
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The phrase "reflection-on-action" refers to:'
    Options:
    - Thinking while teaching
    - Thinking after teaching has occurred
    - Planning before teaching
    - Ignoring past experiences
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: What is a significant barrier to promoting reflective teaching?
    Options:
    - Lack of time and a culture of performance pressure
    - Too much collaboration among staff
    - An overabundance of professional development opportunities
    - Teachers' inherent inability to reflect
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher thinks, "The group work was chaotic because I didn''t
      set clear roles. Next time, I will assign specific tasks." This is an example
      of:'
    Options:
    - Justification of action
    - Blaming students
    - Surface-level reflection
    - Descriptive and analytical reflection leading to an action plan
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which tool is specifically designed to structure reflection by
      moving through stages like Description, Feelings, Evaluation, and Analysis?
    Options:
    - SWOT Analysis
    - Gibbs' Reflective Cycle
    - SMART Goals
    - Bloom's Taxonomy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of the "reflective practitioner" was extensively popularized
      by:'
    Options:
    - Benjamin Bloom
    - B.F. Skinner
    - "Donald Sch\xF6n"
    - Lee Shulman
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'In a peer observation focused on reflection, the observer''s primary
      role is to:'
    Options:
    - Grade the teacher's performance
    - Provide a judgment of "good" or "bad"
    - Collect descriptive data and act as a critical friend
    - Take over the class if needed
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'Reflective teaching is essential in the 21st century primarily
      because it:'
    Options:
    - Is a mandatory requirement for teacher certification
    - Helps teachers adapt to diverse and rapidly changing classrooms
    - Makes lesson planning faster
    - Reduces the need for professional development
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'When a teacher questions the underlying assumptions and power
      structures within the school curriculum, they are engaging in:'
    Options:
    - Technical reflection
    - Practical reflection
    - Critical reflection
    - Deliberative reflection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is an outcome of effective reflective practice?
    Options:
    - Increased rigidity in teaching methods
    - Defensiveness about one's teaching style
    - Enhanced pedagogical decision-making
    - Dependence on external validation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A Professional Learning Community (PLC) supports reflective teaching
      by:'
    Options:
    - Isolating teachers in their classrooms
    - Creating a forum for collaborative inquiry and shared responsibility
    - Focusing solely on administrative announcements
    - Enforcing a standardized teaching method for all
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The first step in most reflective cycles is typically:'
    Options:
    - Developing an action plan
    - Describing the experience
    - Analyzing the causes
    - Evaluating the outcomes
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who only asks, "What went well today?" without probing
      deeper is likely engaging in:'
    Options:
    - Critical reflection
    - Technical rationality
    - Superficial reflection
    - Action research
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'Analyzing student assessment data to identify patterns and adjust
      instruction is a form of:'
    Options:
    - Reflection-in-action
    - Evidence-based reflection
    - Intuitive teaching
    - Summative evaluation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: "Donald Sch\xF6n's concept of \"reflection-in-action\" refers to
      the ability to:"
    Options:
    - Think carefully after a lesson is over
    - Think on your feet and adjust your teaching in the moment
    - Write a detailed lesson plan
    - Reflect only during scheduled times
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A strategy that involves collecting and selecting artifacts to
      demonstrate professional growth is:'
    Options:
    - Peer observation
    - Maintaining a teaching portfolio
    - Critical incident analysis
    - Micro-teaching
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'For reflection to be meaningful, it must be:'
    Options:
    - Always positive and uncritical
    - Based solely on personal feelings
    - Done in isolation
    - Linked to future action and change
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A school leader who wants to foster reflection should primarily:'
    Options:
    - Only reward high test scores
    - Create a safe environment for sharing and risk-taking
    - Publicly criticize teaching failures
    - Mandate a single reflective model for all
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a product of reflective teaching rather
      than a process?
    Options:
    - Journaling
    - Peer observation
    - A revised and improved lesson plan
    - Asking "why" questions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The "Analysis" stage in a reflective model involves:'
    Options:
    - Simply listing what happened
    - Explaining why things happened the way they did
    - Making a value judgment
    - Planning for the next lesson
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher reflecting at a practical level would be concerned with:'
    Options:
    - The efficiency of a teaching technique
    - The educational consequences of their actions and their underlying assumptions
    - The political and ethical implications of the curriculum
    - The alignment with national standards
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which strategy is most focused on generating actionable knowledge
      for a specific classroom context?
    Options:
    - Attending a national conference
    - Reading a theoretical book on education
    - Conducting action research
    - Listening to a motivational speaker
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A key difference between a reflective teacher and a non-reflective
      teacher is that the reflective teacher:'
    Options:
    - Never makes mistakes
    - Views challenges as opportunities for learning
    - Blames external factors for failures
    - Follows the textbook page by page
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The ultimate beneficiary of a teacher''s reflective practice is
      the:'
    Options:
    - School principal
    - Teacher educator
    - Student
    - Teacher themselves
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of these is an essential component of a reflective journal
      entry?
    Options:
    - A record of daily attendance
    - Critical questioning of one's own decisions
    - Copies of all worksheets used
    - Minutes of staff meetings
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The term "critical friend" in reflective practice refers to a
      peer who:'
    Options:
    - Only offers praise
    - Provides supportive yet challenging feedback
    - Focuses on finding faults
    - Avoids giving any opinion
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'Reflective teaching requires teachers to be:'
    Options:
    - Infallible experts
    - Passive recipients of knowledge
    - Active, self-directed learners
    - Detached from their students
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher uses a student survey to gather feedback on their teaching
      methods. This is an example of using:'
    Options:
    - Intuition
    - External evidence for reflection
    - Summative assessment
    - Administrative data
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The habit of reflection helps a teacher to move from a focus on
      what they teach to a focus on:'
    Options:
    - How quickly they can finish the syllabus
    - The number of activities completed
    - How they are perceived by colleagues
    - What and how students are learning
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: Which of the following best describes the relationship between
      reflection and professional development?
    Options:
    - Reflection is a separate activity from professional development.
    - Reflection is the core of continuous professional development.
    - Professional development makes reflection unnecessary.
    - Reflection is only for novice teachers.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher considering whether their assessment methods are fair
      to all students, including those from marginalized groups, is engaging in:'
    Options:
    - Technical reflection
    - Practical reflection
    - Critical reflection
    - Deliberative reflection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'The most significant factor in determining whether a reflective
      strategy will be adopted by teachers is its:'
    Options:
    - Complexity
    - Perceived usefulness and practicality
    - Cost of implementation
    - Popularity in other countries
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
  - QuestionText: 'A reflective teaching culture in a school is one where:'
    Options:
    - Mistakes are hidden
    - Inquiry, dialogue, and shared learning are valued
    - Individual competition is encouraged
    - Teachers work in isolation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: reflective teaching
